 #
-# THE STAGING, AND WHY IT IS NOT "RUN EVERYTHING EVERYWHERE"
-#   pre-commit  fast and offline: format, lint. Seconds.
-#   pre-push    medium: types and tests, still offline.
-#   CI          the full gate, including the network checks.
-#
-# The hook nobody runs is worse than the slower hook that still catches things.
-# A pre-commit needing Databricks auth fails on a plane, and a hook that fails on
-# a plane is a hook people bypass with --no-verify -- at which point it catches
-# nothing at all. A local commit cannot invalidate server-side state, so there is
-# nothing lost by deferring those checks.
-#
-# CI IS DELIBERATELY BROADER THAN THESE HOOKS, NOT A MIRROR OF THEM. A pre-commit
-# hook only ever sees the incoming change, and --no-verify skips it entirely. CI
-# is the layer where a bypass is caught, which only works if it checks more.
-#
-# SYNTAX: `jobs:`, not `commands:`. `commands:` is lefthook 1.x; this repo's
-# lefthook comes from the flake and is 2.x. The exact version is not restated
-# here: a number copied into prose is the one that drifted.
+# pre-commit runs audit:workflows too, restored with the workflow audit and the first workflow.
 
 pre-commit:
